## Intro

Welcome, everyone—to Habyt's Tong Lau, which tonight alone offers passage into _Grand Hotel Europa_.

A novel by the Dutch author _Ilja Leonard Pfeijffer_, often described as _layered_, _baroque_, and _kaleidoscopic_. And while these words are apt, they don’t do justice to the actual _experience_ of reading it.

The book is **autofictive**: the author tells the story as if it were his own, weaving facts from his life with fantasies from his pen.

It is also **metafictive**: it is acutely aware of its own creation and existence as a book.

The combination lends the reading experience a **_Droste effect_** – you can inspect the cans around you _see_ what that means - but that’s to say: The book is recursive!

- The **plot** follows the author's own journey of writing this very book.
- The **setting** shifts between a hotel long past its glory days and a Venice overrun by tourists oblivious to its past—both stand-ins for Europe itself.
- The **characters** dissect the author's erudite European persona: some wax poetic about the beauty and wisdom of the old continent, others expose how lethal the ego and sexism of the old world can be.

As a reader, you become the protagonist in many of his vignettes. After all, who hasn't travelled lusting after Europe's withering treasures? You'll recognize your own current affairs in the fictive debates, and see that the aesthetic and cultural merit we risk losing lives on in the very pages you hold.

Every social commentary must _feel_ relevant to resonate. But thanks to the _Droste effect_, this book pulls you deeper, and deeper, only to jettison you up, out and breaking through the narrative construct of the book – it recurses in reverse! - out into your psyche, out into our world, and demands that you see Europe, travel, and the value of culture anew. It’s quite the trip!

The theme of european identity spans the book, and surfaces explicitly over a dinner - that is the excerpt we read tonight.

In keeping of the dazzling references in and outside of fiction, it’s a discussion on George Steiner’s idea for what it means to be European. The discussion is between Ilya, the protagonist, and Patelski, his intellectual sparring partner and fellow hotel resident.

Now, I know it may feel like a bait-and-switch to read from one book only to discuss another. But I promise you: this is precisely how the _Droste effect_ takes you places.

## Reading

The main course was served. It was steak Paganini.

**Ilja**:

‘The Greeks were spared the fate of a revelation,’ I said. ‘Their religion and myths were never set down in an authoritative holy book.

That’s why the foundations of their belief have remained susceptible to discussion.

When there are no dictates from a god, it isn’t sacrilegious to think for yourself.

This is the birth of Greek philosophy.’

**Patelski**:

‘You’ve already written that somewhere. I remember it.

What is unique about European civilization is that this reason that has been kissed awake, then has had to come to terms with a revelation that claims to be the absolute truth.

In that respect, the epicentre of European history can be sought in the actions of the Church Fathers who took it upon themselves to make their unbelievable belief socially acceptable to the heathen intelligentsia by having it chime with the illustrious achievements of Greek philosophy, using arguments of surreal beauty.

The fact that the European painting tradition put to use the rules of perspective developed by Leonardo da Vinci with utter scientific precision, to create images of devotion, is, if you wish, a splendid illustration of the same paradoxical tradition.’

**Ilja**:

‘I would dare to question,’ I said, ‘whether the struggle between reason and religion is an exclusively European thing. Perhaps it is more of a general human obsession.’

**Patelski**:

‘The point is the coexistence of two formidable traditions.

I think that Europe doesn’t only have the exclusive right to the combination of the two, but also to each thing separately.

With the exception of Islam, religious experience outside of Europe is not based on a revelation, and with the exception of India and China, the philosophical tradition outside of Europe is not a coherent discourse between written texts.

I’m exaggerating somewhat but not to an unusual extent.

In Europe both traditions are scholarly.

As far as I’m concerned you can call that unique.’

**Ilja**:

‘So it’s ultimately about books then,’ I said.

‘I’m delighted that not just my pub visits but also my choice of career as a writer betray a European mentality.’

**Patelski**:

‘As long as you’re aware of that,’ he said.

**Ilja**:

‘It’s just a shame that fewer and fewer readers are aware of that.’

**Patelski**:

‘This brings us to the fifth and final characteristic of the European identity,’ said Patelski. ‘Europe is aware of its own decay.’

**Ilja**:

‘Just as man is the only living being who is aware of his own mortality, Europe is the only continent that experiences its own decline and predicts its own tragic destruction.

It’s the flipside of a rich past.

If you’ve known a glorious past, you soon come to the idea that the best times are behind you and that there’s only a minimal chance that things will ever be the way they were again.’

**Patelski**:

‘It’s more deeply rooted than that,’ he said.

‘Over the course of its history, Europe has seen so many powerful empires collapse that the cycle of emergence, blossoming and decay has nestled itself as a model in the deepest cerebral cortex of our historical consciousness.

We experience this tripartite template, including in our eyes the inevitable decline as aesthetically satisfying, like symmetry, the three-part construction of an argument as introduction, exposition and conclusion, an altarpiece that was designed as a triptych or the sonata form which consists of exposition, development and recapitulation.

We see beauty in decadence because we idealize the examples of this from the past like the glorious grandiose decline of the Roman empire, and because for us decay is part of a well-rounded structure which, without the last part of the triptych, would be aesthetically crippled and remain lacking.’

**Ilja**:

‘If your analysis is correct,’ I said, ‘it’s a good thing that Europe’s decline is actually demonstrable.

Although we like to confine our attention to the continent’s cultural achievements, we know full well that Europe was only able to flourish thanks to a solid foundation of economic and military superiority.

And while we don’t want to stop talking about those cultural achievements, in economic and military terms, Europe is definitively and irreversibly outdated in comparison to large parts of the rest of the world.

That’s why Europe has lost its authority.

And its moral authority, which we like to rely on for lack of anything better, is a fiction as long as we are divided amongst ourselves and fail to speak with one harmonious voice.

And even then. The rest of the world has nothing to gain from our self-invented moral authority because we can no longer force them to listen to us using weapons and ducats.

Europe has become irrelevant on the world stage and has lost its influence on the future.

Days go by without the Chinese thinking about us, I think I can dare to claim.

Europe doesn’t produce anything.

All the objects that pass through our hands on a daily basis are made in China. Our clothes come from India and Bangladesh and our dreams from Hollywood.

The last languishing factories on our continent are being kept open just a little longer by trade unions and despair out of misplaced nostalgia, until they too will be added as industrial heritage to our endless list of monuments that testify to better times.

We have rigged up a refined and subtle service economy to facilitate Chinese enrichment on our continent and administer our own demise.

But in fact, we have nothing left to sell but our past.’

**Patelski**:

‘You speak like a true European,’ he said. ‘Not only your bar frequentations and career choice testify to a European disposition, doom-mongering is also in your blood.’

**Ilja**:

‘Don’t you agree with me then?’

**Patelski**:

‘No, I’m paying you a compliment.’

**Ilja**:

‘Perhaps you will allow me, with thanks to your agreement and your word of praise, to permit myself a small note of criticism.

The worrying thing about thoughts of decline is that these days they mainly resonate with followers of extreme right-wing prophets who blame the demise of European culture on the dissipation of Judaeo-Christian and humanist values under pressure from the Islamisation of the continent as a consequence of mass migration.

I wouldn’t like to entitle my book on this matter _The Decline of the Occident_ because of running the risk that it would be all too eagerly picked up thanks to the popularity of right-wing thought.’

**Patelski**:

‘It is to your credit that you worry about the sales of your books,’ he said.

‘But I don’t think it’s difficult to draw a distinction between defensive nostalgia, which manifests itself in a longing to return to the Middle Ages, when stout-hearted knights slaughtered infidels in the name of the cross, and historical realism which realizes that Europe’s only hope for the future lies in far-reaching integration, federalization and unity, and that the arrival of young, strong, resilient immigrants is a gift to the old, greying continent as opposed to a threat.’

**Ilja**:

‘I’m delighted you dare to speak of hope and the future,’ I said.

‘On the grounds of the five characteristics of the European identity that we’ve just discussed, I can tell you what the future of Europe looks like.

The future at this moment is already present and operational on a large scale.

What will become of our cafés and bars, our accessible nature, our excess of past, our culture of reason and revelation and our talent for decadence, is what has already happened to it.

Europe has become a theme park.

The once so feared Black Forest is a signposted rambling area.

If you want to climb Mont Blanc you have to buy a ticket.

For visitors who would like to cool off, we have the photogenic, ridiculously blue Mediterranean inland sea, free of the sharks, waves and other dangers that characterize a real sea.

All of this bathed in a pleasant climate, stripped of extremes.

And this park is dotted with visitor attractions.

You can marvel at monuments from a past that appeal to the imagination, and you won’t tire of cathedrals that reason built for religion.

It has all been beautifully restored and maintained because every European realizes that we are on the verge of collapse and the past is all we have.

Europe has become an open air museum, a fantastic historical park for tourists.

And the primary ingredient of our tourist-welcoming destiny is, according to the first and most important characteristic of our identity, our exceptional catering industry.

The ubiquity of cafés and bars and the wealth of formidable culinary traditions makes our continent the ideal tourist destination.

Europe’s future is the Europe that is now a reality.

Europe is the rest of the world’s recreation area.’

**Patelski**:

‘The question is whether that’s a bad thing,’ he said.

**Ilja**:

‘The question,’ I repeated, ‘is whether that’s a bad thing.’

## Outro

No excerpt of this book does justice to the majesty of the Grand Europa Hotel. That only emerges once all threads wrap up and you’ve fully gone through the Droste Effect, but for that experience… you will have to read the book. Thank you for listening.
